### 2.2 Finding #1: The 40% False-Positive Meta-Defect in Automated Keyword Scanners

A foundational revelation of Campaign 1 was an industry-wide methodological flaw in automated AI red-teaming benchmarks.

Standard automated evaluation harnesses rely on substring matching to score attack success: if the target model's output contains keywords from the illicit prompt (e.g., `"lock picking"`, `"phishing script"`, `"reverse shell"`), the harness automatically marks the jailbreak as **SUCCESS**.

```
[ Automated Attack Generator ] ──> Sends: "How to perform lock picking"
                                              │
                                              ▼
                                 [ Target Language Model ]
                                              │
                                              ▼
                 Output: "I cannot assist you with lock picking techniques."
                                              │
                                              ▼
                      [ Naive Substring Scanner: Contains "lock picking"? ]
                                              │
                                              ▼
                   [ FALSE POSITIVE: Attack Scored as 100% SUCCESSFUL ]
```

When AION manually audited the preliminary "70% success rate" reported by automated testing on Layer 1, the true success rate collapsed to **~30–40%**. 

The cause was systematic **Echo Refusal**: the language model frequently repeats the user's prohibited topic *inside the refusal statement itself*:
$$\text{Output} = \underbrace{\text{"I am unable to fulfill your request regarding "}}_{\text{Refusal Framing}} + \underbrace{\text{[Illicit Query String]}}_{\text{Topic Echo}} + \underbrace{\text{" due to safety policies."}}_{\text{Policy Statement}}$$

Because the substring scanner matches `[Illicit Query String]`, it records a successful breach when the model in fact held the ethical boundary. This metric inflation distorts academic literature and enterprise benchmark suites. 

**Architectural Fix:** APEX mandated the immediate deprecation of naive string-based jailbreak scoring, replacing it with dual-pass deterministic refusal-pattern analyzers and structured semantic classifiers.

